---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import { SITE } from '../data/site';
---

<Base
  title="About"
  description="What Open Tech Art is, how resources are selected, and the principles behind the catalog."
  section="about"
>
  <div class="wrap page">
    <header>
      <p class="eyebrow">About</p>
      <h1>An open library for technical art</h1>
    </header>
    <div class="prose">
      <p class="lead">
        A lot of great technical art is shared openly on GitHub, but it's scattered across thousands of
        repositories with inconsistent READMEs and few screenshots. Open Tech Art collects the good, openly
        licensed work into one consistent, searchable catalog, and it always sends you back to the
        repository and the people who made it.
      </p>

      <h2>What this is — and isn't</h2>
      <p>
        Open Tech Art is a <strong>curated resource library</strong>, not a marketplace. Nothing here is for
        sale, there are no ratings or paid placements, and there are no accounts. Every entry points to a
        public GitHub repository. For each one we add discovery, consistent metadata, screenshots,
        categorisation, compatibility notes and a clear link to the original project.
      </p>
      <p>
        The library starts with <strong>Unity URP</strong> shaders and rendering techniques, especially
        Shader Graph. The content model is deliberately broader, so VFX, lighting setups, impostor systems,
        reference scenes, editor tools and other engines can be added without restructuring.
      </p>

      <h2>Selection criteria</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>The project lives in a <strong>public GitHub repository</strong>.</li>
        <li>It is <strong>free</strong> to obtain.</li>
        <li>
          It has an <strong>explicit open license</strong> that allows use and the display of its
          screenshots, such as MIT, BSD, Apache 2.0, CC0, CC BY or the Unlicense. Free of charge is not the
          same as open, and anything with ambiguous terms is left out.
        </li>
        <li>The creator provides screenshots that we are allowed to show.</li>
        <li>Compatibility is recorded only as stated by the creator or evident from the project.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        Every entry has a <em>catalog record</em>: the date it was checked, the revision that was checked,
        and notes on how. If something has changed upstream, please
        <a href={SITE.issuesUrl}>open an issue</a> or edit the entry directly.
      </p>

      <h2 id="github-stats">Stars, updates and featured picks</h2>
      <p>
        Each entry shows its repository's GitHub star count and the date of its latest commit, so you can
        tell at a glance whether a project is popular and still maintained. You can sort the catalog by
        either. <strong>Featured</strong> picks combine the two: well-starred repositories rank higher,
        and that score fades the longer a repository goes without a commit. These numbers are refreshed
        by a maintainer running a script, so they can be a little behind GitHub.
      </p>

      <h2 id="raw-reference-showcase">Raw → Reference → Showcase</h2>
      <p>Our long-term image standard has three parts:</p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Raw</strong> shows what goes in: the geometry or input without the technique.</li>
        <li><strong>Reference</strong> shows what the technique itself does, under simple, honest conditions.</li>
        <li><strong>Showcase</strong> shows what can be achieved with it in a polished scene.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        Resource pages can already show Raw and Reference side by side or with a comparison slider. The
        first seed entries were collected from the web, so they use the creators' own screenshots. We never
        manufacture missing images. Unusual techniques sometimes need exceptions, because honesty and
        clarity matter more than rigid standardisation.
      </p>

      <h2>Licensing of this site</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>The website source code is released under the MIT License.</li>
        <li>
          Catalog metadata written for Open Tech Art (descriptions, tags, structured fields) is dedicated to
          the public domain under CC0 1.0. You can reuse it freely, for example through
          <a href="/resources.json">the JSON catalog</a>.
        </li>
        <li>
          Resources and their screenshots remain the work of their creators. They are shown under each
          resource's own license, which is stated on its page.
        </li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        If you are a creator and want an entry changed or removed, <a href={SITE.issuesUrl}>open an issue</a>
        and we'll act on it promptly.
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>
</Base>

<style>
  .page {
    padding-top: 40px;
  }
  header h1 {
    font-size: clamp(1.9rem, 3.4vw, 2.7rem);
    max-width: 22ch;
    margin: 8px 0 24px;
  }
  .lead {
    font-size: 1.15rem;
    color: var(--ink);
  }
</style>
